Every day, radiologists review hundreds of scans to find out whether a patient has cancer. The number of scans keeps growing, but the number of radiologists does not. AI can help. For prostate cancer, AI systems can now read MRI scans about as well as experienced radiologists. Yet today a radiologist still checks every scan, so the AI saves little time.
That could change if AI were allowed to handle the clear-cut cases on its own, leaving radiologists more time for the difficult ones. But a hospital is not a static environment. New scanners are installed, scan settings change and the patient […] opulation shifts over time. Each of these changes can make an AI system less accurate without anyone noticing. Radiologists who work with AI every day may also start to trust it more than they should. European law now requires hospitals and manufacturers to keep monitoring AI once it is in use, but nobody has yet shown how to do that reliably in practice.
In this PhD project, funded by Stichting Hanarth Fonds, you will work out how to monitor AI safely in daily clinical practice, using prostate MRI as a starting point. You will:
- develop and validate statistical methods that detect drops in AI performance early, using a multicenter dataset of more than 10,000 prostate MRI examinations collected over twelve years;
- design and run an international reader study that measures how strongly AI advice influences radiologists;
- translate these findings into a monitoring protocol together with international experts, aligned with the EU AI Act;
- test the protocol prospectively in a multicenter study at UMCG and Radboudumc on more than 500 new examinations, in parallel with routine care;
- publish your work in international journals and present it at conferences, leading to a PhD thesis.
